  "query_preview": "..Current satellite imagery show a plume of moisture \noriginating from the remnants of Odalys moving across northern \nArizona. Meanwhile, Hurricane Polo is centered of the west coast \nof Southern Baja California. Moisture associated with these two \nfeatures will interact with a closed low approaching northern \nArizona, which is currently centered over Central California. This\nactive weather pattern is forecast to bring a period of impactful\nweather to the area through Wednesday.\n\nRadar imagery shows a band of primarily stratiform precipitation\ndraped across northern Arizona from the southwest to northeast. \nRain rates are mostly light, with some embedded showers producing\nmoderate rainfall rates. With the cool and cloudy conditions, we \nare not expecting robust storm development today, but some \nisolated storm activity cannot be ruled out this afternoon. The \nmain concern is for flooding in areas that receive prolonged \nperiods of moderate to heavy rainfall. Otherwise, today's forecast\ncalls for light winds with daytime temperatures around 10 to 20 \ndegrees below normal. \n\nFrom late tonight through Wednesday morning, the low is forecast\nto move across northern Arizona. As it does so, additional\natmospheric moisture from Hurricane Polo will be entrained into\nthe low. With increased synoptic-scale forcing associated with the\npassing low, greater storm activity and higher rainfall rates are\nforecast. High-resolution model guidance highlights the eastern \nMogollon Rim and White Mountains as the area with the highest \nflood risk with 24 hour rainfall totals up to 3 inches. We will \nalso see higher 0-6km Bulk Wind Shear, with values ranging from \n40-60 kts. Despite the strong shear, forecast CAPE values at or \nbelow 600 J/kg may limit the overall severe thunderstorm threat, \nthough isolated severe storms cannot be ruled out given the\nbenefit of forcing from the low. So, all things considered, we \nare looking at a moderate/high risk of flooding/flash flooding, \nwith a lower risk of strong storms producing large hail and severe\nwind gusts. A Flood Watch remains in effect for northern Arizona \nthrough midnight Tuesday.\n\nOn Wednesday, the low center moves into New Mexico, with chances\nfor showers and storms primarily remaining across eastern \nArizona. High pressure is forecast to bring a return of dry \nweather, with daytime temperatures warming to near average on \nThursday and Friday.",
